+{"id": "README-010", "title": "P4 — README claims CI's Go 1.26 toolchain is sufficient while go.mod requires Go 1.26.10", "status": "complete", "priority": "P4", "reasoning": "Doc severity P4 by Bane's 2026-09-30 ruling: documentation findings are P4/P5, not P1-P3; this policy is not a new assessment of the underlying toolchain risk.\n\nFINDING: README.md:1493-1501 says the toolchain requirement is `go 1.26.10`, but .github/workflows/ci.yml:15 runs the full CI job with `go-version: [\"1.26\"]`. On GitHub Actions, setup-go resolves that moving minor line independently of the README's patch requirement, so the README does not tell a stranger the actual CI toolchain floor or how CI obtains 1.26.10.\n\nEXACT COMMANDS AND OUTPUT:\n`grep -nE 'go-version|go 1\\.26' README.md .github/workflows/ci.yml go.mod` -> README.md:1493: The toolchain requirement: go.mod declares `go 1.26.10`; .github/workflows/ci.yml:15: go-version: [\"1.26\"]; go.mod:3: go 1.26.10.\n`sed -n '12,28p' .github/workflows/ci.yml` -> CI configures setup-go with `go-version: [\"1.26\"]`, not `1.26.10`.\n`go version` -> go1.26.10 linux/amd64 for this local probe; no older Go toolchain was installed to exercise the refusal branch.\n\nFIX: reconcile the README and CI contract: either pin CI to `1.26.10` and state that exact floor, or document the moving `1.26` CI resolution and separately explain how the patched 1.26.10 requirement is enforced. Add a reproducible parity check so the two files cannot drift.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the README toolchain paragraph, go.mod:3, and CI setup-go version express one verified policy; a fresh checkout can run the documented command and see the same floor.\n\nUNVERIFIED: no older Go 1.26.x toolchain was available, so this sweep did not prove the exact failure behavior below 1.26.10.\n\nch:trace row=README-010 spec=README.md#deployment-freshness-gate-bt-057 wave=none:serial-tick test=none:no-test-covers-readme-ci-toolchain doc=README.md prompt=~/.hermes/skills/coding-hermes/coding