+ {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-50", "title": "no boardctl verb can set row acceptance_criteria — the fleet's dispatchability requirement forces every agent to hand-edit tasks.jsonl in raw JSON, bypassing the id/shape guards and the BT-050 lock", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25 (tick boardctl-dogfood-2026-10-23-10-52-34), second delivery. Found while filing this run's own rows: the fleet's foremen will not dispatch a row without acceptance_criteria, but there is no flag to write it.\n\nEVIDENCE: `boardctl create --id TEST-AA-1 --title t --acceptance-criteria 'must do x'` -> rc=1, 'flag provided but not defined: -acceptance-criteria'. `boardctl update --help` has no acceptance flag either (34 flags listed, none named acceptance). README mentions acceptance_criteria only as a DRIFT KEY the normalize-board repair rewrites (README:476, 811, 823) — the tool can repair that key but cannot author it. This run's rows and the sibling delivery's DF-BOARDCTL-44..47 all had their criteria appended 'line-wise' by a python JSONL edit, i.e. exactly the bypass README:1262-1264 warns about ('Appending rows with your own JSON serialization bypasses the id-refusal and shape guards AND this lock — that bypass is the known source of duplicate-id corruption on fleet boards').\n\nConsequence: the sanctioned workflow has no sanctioned path for a required field, so every lane takes the unsanctioned one; the resulting interleave is exactly what produced the duplicate-id corruption BT-050 was written to stop. Secondary: the same gap exists for other foreman-consumed fields (e.g. capability_tags is settable at create, acceptance is not), so a row can be created dispatchable-looking but undispatched.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:26:21Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-24T04:56:33Z", "created_by": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1) A dispatchable row must be authorable through the locked write path: `boardctl create --id TEST-AA-1 --title t` plus the new acceptance flag must e